 $485K for Singer) with no outside spending against him", "Expert ratings split between Lean D and Tossup, with markets pricing the Democrat around 70-76%", "Singer has run a well-funded challenge with Chamber backing and a crossover endorsement, keeping the race competitive", "The district leans Republican on partisanship, but the national environment favors Democrats"], "rationale": "Moskowitz's financial edge and a favorable national environment offset the district's Republican lean, and most raters and markets still put him ahead. Singer's strong fundraising and late endorsements justify trimming the expected margin slightly, but not enough to move the race to Tossup.", "watch": "Super PAC spending on airwaves in the final weeks, and whether Singer's crossover and business-community endorsements translate into movement among moderate voters.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Jared Moskowitz (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Jared Moskowitz defends a South Florida seat that leans slightly Republican on paper but has trended competitive. He enters the fall with a major financial advantage, roughly $1.9 million on hand against Scott Singer's $485,000, and no outside groups running against him. Singer, a Boca Raton official, has raised over $2.2 million and drawn support from outside groups, the U.S. Chamber, and a Democratic crossover endorsement, keeping Republicans invested in the race.\n\nExpert ratings are split between Lean D and Tossup, and prediction markets price Moskowitz's chances around 70 to 76 percent. Polling is sparse and dated, with May surveys showing Moskowitz ahead by double digits but no recent public numbers. The race has centered on immigration, gun policy, and cost of living, with both candidates on television. Moskowitz is favored, but the margin of error is wide.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7798, "marg